# Nightly dump of the aiku production database (INI-071). Two hosts: helio makes
# the dump; boro checks every day that one arrived, so a dead helio or a dead cron
# is noticed too, and puts an encrypted copy of .env and the OAuth keys on the
# storage box for helio to send to R2 with the dump.
#
# On BOTH hosts (root):
#     install -m 755 devops/postgres/aiku-db-backup.sh /usr/local/bin/aiku-db-backup
#     /usr/local/bin/aiku-db-backup --self-test     # logic, no side effects
#     /usr/local/bin/aiku-db-backup --test          # posts ONE real Discord message
#
# On HELIO, keep the first and the last schedule lines; on BORO the two in between:
#     install -m 644 devops/cron/aiku-db-backup /etc/cron.d/aiku-db-backup
#
# The dump is read from the delayed replica (port 5435, devops/postgres/helio-delayed.conf),
# so it puts no load on boro or litio and holds the data as it was 12 hours earlier.
# It is written to /var/lib/postgresql/backups first (the newest one stays there for a
# fast restore), then copied to /sel/aiku/db on the storage box: 7 daily and 4 weekly
# (Sunday) dumps, each with the roles in a .globals.sql and its checksum in a
# .sha256 beside it. The copy on the storage box is read back and compared with the
# original before it counts. The same files and the same rotation go to Cloudflare
# R2, so one copy sits outside Hetzner.
#
# R2 (helio only): a bucket of its own, private, and an API token limited to that
# bucket with Object Read & Write, never the app's token. Then, as root:
#     apt install rclone
#     rclone config create r2 s3 provider=Cloudflare access_key_id=... secret_access_key=... \
#         endpoint=https://<account id>.eu.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# and keep the AIKU_BACKUP_R2 line below. Without it the R2 copy is skipped.
# The bucket is in the EU jurisdiction, hence ".eu." in the endpoint; the plain
# endpoint does not see it. It carries a 7 day lock (no delete, no overwrite, for
# any token), so the oldest daily is sometimes removed a night late.
#
# .env and the OAuth keys (boro only) are encrypted with a public key, so no server
# can open the copies. The private key is kept off the servers, by Raul:
#     gpg --quick-generate-key aiku-backup default default never
#     gpg --export aiku-backup > aiku-backup.pub                       # goes to /etc/aiku-backup.pub on boro
#     gpg --export-secret-keys --armor aiku-backup                     # into the password manager
# To open a copy:  gpg --decrypt aiku-secrets-YYYY-MM-DD.tar.gpg | tar -xf -
# Without APP_KEY from that .env the columns the app encrypts cannot be read from a dump.
#
# helio's own configuration (INI-086: its disks are worn out and it must be rebuildable
# in hours) goes the same way as helio-config-YYYY-MM-DD.tar.gpg, to the storage box and
# R2: /etc, /usr/local, the crontabs, root's and postgres's ssh keys (storage box,
# pgBackRest), the rclone R2 token and the staging .env files. Copy the public key from
# boro first:  scp boro:/etc/aiku-backup.pub /etc/aiku-backup.pub
# Rebuilding helio from it: devops/helio-rebuild.md.
#
# This does not replace /home/inikoo/backup_aiku.sh, which
# /etc/crontab on boro runs at 03:30 and which overwrites one aiku.dump every night.
# That job stays: its aiku_latest.dump, taken from the primary, is the copy developers
# download, and a dump from here would be 12 hours older.
#
# Restore, onto an empty database (first: sha256sum -c aiku-YYYY-MM-DD.dump.sha256):
#     psql -f aiku-YYYY-MM-DD.globals.sql postgres
#     createdb -O aiku aiku && pg_restore -j 8 -d aiku aiku-YYYY-MM-DD.dump
#
# The clocks differ: helio is on Europe/Berlin and starts at 01:30 there (07:30 on
# boro, Asia/Kuala_Lumpur); boro checks at 12:00 its own time, 4.5 hours later.
#
# AWS S3 (boro only, INI-133): the encrypted secrets also go to the bucket that holds the
# continuous database backup, so a server started at AWS after losing Hetzner finds
# everything it needs in one place, read with its own read-only role. boro's pgBackRest key
# may write there (IP-locked to boro and litio); an rclone remote with the same key, as root:
#     rclone config create aws s3 provider=AWS region=eu-central-1 location_constraint=eu-central-1 \
#         access_key_id=... secret_access_key=... no_check_bucket=true
# (values from /etc/pgbackrest/conf.d/aws.conf). --check then also alerts when today's
# file did not reach S3.
#
# Override per host as lines above the schedule. Helio has no production .env and
# its staging .env has no DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL, so on helio put that one line in
# /etc/aiku-alerts.env (root, mode 600) and keep the override below; on boro delete
# it. The same file fixes helio's disk alert: add DISK_ALERT_ENV_FILE=/etc/aiku-alerts.env
# to /etc/cron.d/disk-alert there, which today reads a path that does not exist.
AIKU_BACKUP_ENV_FILE=/etc/aiku-alerts.env
AIKU_BACKUP_R2=r2:aiku-db-backups
30 1 * * * root /usr/local/bin/aiku-db-backup
0 12 * * * root AIKU_BACKUP_S3=aws:aiku-pgbackrest/aiku-secrets /usr/local/bin/aiku-db-backup --check
0 3 * * * root AIKU_BACKUP_S3=aws:aiku-pgbackrest/aiku-secrets /usr/local/bin/aiku-db-backup --secrets
0 2 * * * root AIKU_BACKUP_SECRETS_NAME=helio-config AIKU_BACKUP_SECRETS_SOURCE=/ AIKU_BACKUP_SECRETS_FILES="etc usr/local var/spool/cron root/.ssh root/.config/rclone var/lib/postgresql/.ssh home/staging/aiku/shared/.env home/staging/aiku/anchor/octane/.env" /usr/local/bin/aiku-db-backup --secrets
